# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hoverlab environment
#
# Copy to .env and fill in. Everything except the Firebase keys is optional —
# the app runs with analytics and billing switched off when their keys are
# absent, rather than erroring.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Required: Firebase -----------------------------------------------------
#
# Accounts, sign-in and password reset are Firebase Auth; favourites, bundles
# and billing records live in Firestore.
#
# Note where the work happens: the SERVER calls Firebase, not the browser.
# The Firebase client SDK talks to identitytoolkit.googleapis.com directly,
# which makes sign-in fail for anyone running an ad blocker, a privacy
# extension or filtered DNS — a failure the app cannot detect or fix. Sign-in
# is server-side for that reason, so only these two values are needed.
#
# Setup (console.firebase.google.com):
#   1. Create a project.
#   2. Authentication → Sign-in method → enable Email/Password.
#   3. Firestore Database → create a database.
#   4. Project settings → General → Web API key → the value below.
#   5. Project settings → Service accounts → Generate new private key. That
#      JSON file IS a secret — it bypasses all security rules.

# Public by design: it identifies the project and is useless without
# credentials. NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY is read as a fallback.
FIREBASE_API_KEY=""

# Server credentials. Use ONE of the following.
#
# Local development — path to the downloaded key file. Keep it out of git;
# .gitignore already covers *service-account*.json.
FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_FILE="./firebase-service-account.json"
#
# Hosting (Vercel) — the same JSON as a single-line string, since there is no
# file to point at. Paste the whole file, starting with {"type":"service_account".
# FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=""


# --- Canonical URL ----------------------------------------------------------
# The origin that canonical tags, OG image URLs, sitemap.xml entries, and the
# post-checkout return URL are all built from. No trailing slash.
#
# ON VERCEL, LEAVE THIS UNSET unless you are serving a custom domain. Without
# it, src/lib/site.ts falls back to VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL — the
# project's own production domain, which is always correct and never goes
# stale. Setting it by hand pins a domain that outlives its DNS: point it at a
# domain you have not attached yet and the sitemap advertises URLs that do not
# resolve, while checkout redirects a customer who has just paid to a dead
# page. Set it only once the domain actually resolves.
#
# Required everywhere else — off Vercel there is nothing to fall back to but
# http://localhost:3000.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="https://hoverlab.dev"


# --- Analytics (PostHog) ----------------------------------------------------
# Omit to disable analytics entirely; no network calls are made without a key.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST="https://us.i.posthog.com"


# --- Billing (Polar) --------------------------------------------------------
# Polar is the merchant of record: it collects and remits VAT / sales tax, so
# no per-jurisdiction tax registration is needed.
#
# Setup:
#  1. Create an organization at https://polar.sh
#  2. Create two products:
#       "Hoverlab Pro"  — one-time payment
#       "Hoverlab Team" — recurring monthly, per-seat
#  3. Copy each product id below.
#  4. Add a webhook endpoint pointing at:
#       https://<your-domain>/api/billing/webhook
#     subscribed to: order.paid, order.refunded, subscription.active,
#     subscription.updated, subscription.canceled, subscription.revoked,
#     subscription.past_due, subscription.uncanceled
#  5. Copy the webhook signing secret below.
#
# Billing routes return 503 until POLAR_ACCESS_TOKEN and POLAR_WEBHOOK_SECRET
# are both set, and the pricing page falls back to a waitlist CTA for any tier
# whose product id is missing.

POLAR_ACCESS_TOKEN=""
POLAR_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""
POLAR_PRODUCT_ID_PRO=""
POLAR_PRODUCT_ID_TEAM=""

# --- Regional (PPP) pricing -------------------------------------------------
# Polar has no native purchasing-power-parity pricing, so a region is a
# DISCOUNT off the USD list price rather than a second product.
#
# Create these under Products → Discounts in the Polar dashboard. Use FIXED
# amounts, not percentages: the prices in src/lib/billing/plans.ts are round
# numbers, and a percentage lands just off them ($59 less 68% is $18.88, not
# $19), so the page would advertise a price the receipt doesn't match.
#   India / Pro   — $40 off, restricted to the Pro product,  no redemption cap
#                   ($59 - $40 = $19, matching REGIONAL.IN.pro)
#   India / Team  — $7 off,  restricted to the Team product, no redemption cap
#                   ($12 - $7  = $5,  matching REGIONAL.IN.team)
#
# Then paste the discount ids below. The country comes from the edge
# geolocation header (x-vercel-ip-country) on the server, never from the
# client, so the displayed price cannot be talked into a cheaper charge.
#
# Leaving one blank is safe: src/lib/billing/plans.ts falls back to the list
# price for display, so a missing id can never advertise $19 and charge $59.
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_IN_PRO=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_IN_TEAM=""

# 'sandbox' (default) or 'production'. Defaults to sandbox so a misconfigured
# deploy cannot accidentally take real money.
POLAR_SERVER="sandbox"
